    "title": "Tyson to acquire old Cargill building in Springdale - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Campbell Roper ,\n3 hours ago\nSPRINGDALE, Ark (KNWA/KFTA) — The Springdale City Council voted in favor of Tyson acquiring the idled Cargill building at 730 E. Randall Wobbe Ln. through the issuance of revenue bonds.\nAccording to documents from the council’s Nov. 17 meeting, Tyson requested an issuance of Act 9 bonds, also known as Industrial Development Revenue Bonds. The documents said the city would issue the bond and Tyson would then acquire the bond.\nThe city approved and adopted the bonds but will incur no cost through the bonds.\nTyson will participate in payments in lieu of taxes, which would allow the corporation to receive 50% of property tax abatement over 10 years, according to Nathan McKay, president of poultry for Tyson Foods.\nMichaels Rogers store to open in early 2026 According to the U.S. Department of the Interior, PILOTs are federal payments that help local governments offset losses in property taxes due to the existence of nontaxable federal lands within their boundaries.\nAccording to Talk Business and Politics , McKay asked the city to issue the bonds to help with costs necessary to convert the plant for chicken processing purposes.\nThe council’s agenda packet included information on $127-million-cost-projections of retrofitting the building.\nTalk Business reported Kutak Rock LLP was appointed as bond counsel with respect to the issuance of the bond. The fees and expenses to Kutak Rock will be covered with proceeds from the bond or by Tyson Foods.\nThe plant opening will provide about 200 jobs, according to Tyson’s proposal.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KNWA FOX24.\n2",
